 not limit the right to obtain a copy of the trust instrument in a judicial proceeding.","execution_requirements":"Wyo. Stat. §4-10-402 requires trust creation by a written instrument or oral trust established by clear and convincing evidence. The trust instrument does not require notarization or witnesses under the UTC framework, though notarization is recommended for practical purposes. Real property transferred to a trust must be recorded with the county clerk. Certification of trust under §4-10-1014 may be signed or otherwise authenticated by any trustee; no notary or witness requirement is specified. Wyoming also permits qualified spendthrift trusts (DAPTs) under §4-10-505, which require a qualified transfer affidavit sworn to by the settlor.","business_trust_treatment":"Wyoming recognizes statutory trusts under the Wyoming Statutory Trust Act (Wyo. Stat. §17-29-101 et seq.), which provides for a flexible business entity similar to a Delaware statutory trust. A Wyoming statutory trust is a separate legal entity with limited liability for beneficial owners and trustees. Common law business trusts (Massachusetts trusts) are also recognized. The Wyoming Uniform Trust Code (Title 4, Chapter 10) applies to express trusts and does not directly govern statutory business trusts. Wyoming's series LLC statutes (§17-29-201 et seq.) also treat LLC series as separate persons, providing additional business trust-like structures.","favorable_trust_laws":"Wyoming has abolished the Rule Against Perpetuities for qualifying trusts, allowing dynasty trusts to last up to 1,000 years—one of the longest non-perpetual periods in the country. Wyoming has no state income tax, making it extremely favorable for trust income accumulation. The state enacted a Domestic Asset Protection Trust (DAPT) statute under §4-10-505 (qualified spendthrift trusts), allowing self-settled asset protection trusts. The 4-year statute of limitations for fraudulent transfer claims (or 1 year from discovery) provides strong creditor protection. Wyoming also provides privacy protections in judicial proceedings involving trusts. The state supports flexible modification mechanisms including decanting, nonjudicial settlement agreements, and court-supervised modification. Trusts can be created for the care of animals under §4-10-410.","unique_factors":"Wyoming combines no state income tax, a 1,000-year dynasty trust period, DAPT legislation, and strong privacy protections, making it one of the most trust-friendly states. The state adopted the UTC in 2003 but has heavily modified it to create a flexible, settlor-friendly framework. Wyoming's qualified spendthrift trust statute (§4-10-505) requires a qualified transfer affidavit sworn to by the settlor, providing an additional layer of procedural protection. The state's series LLC framework also adds unique business structuring flexibility. Wyoming's geographic privacy protections in judicial proceedings are notable—upon filing of any petition, trust instruments and related documents receive privacy protections.","provenance":{"sources":[{"label":"Wyo. Stat. Â§ 4-10-1014","url":"https://wyoleg.gov/statutes/compress/title04.pdf","kind":"official"}],"verified_as_of":"2026-09-17","human_legal_reviewed":false,"evidence":"HTTP 200; PDF contains '4-10-1014. Certification or affidavit of trust.' and the full statute text. The wyoleg.gov statutes SPA (HTML) returns HTTP 200 but is a client-side rendered app that does not expose statute text in the initial HTML; the PDF is the authoritative Tier-1 official source with the statute text verifiable in the PDF."}};
